r's okay with when closing their position. Say, for a random symbol, the market price is $1000. \t\t\t\t\t\tIf a user wants to close a short position on this symbol, they might be cool with prices up to $1010","deadline":"The deadline for executing the position closure. If 'partyB' doesn't get back to the request within a certain time, then the request will just time out","orderType":"orderType can again be LIMIT or MARKET with the same logic as in SendQuote","quantityToClose":"The quantity of the position to be closed.","quoteId":"The ID of the quote associated with the position to be closed."}},"sendQuote(address[],uint256,uint8,uint8,uint256,uint256,uint256,uint256,uint256,uint256,uint256,uint256,(bytes,uint256,int256,uint256,bytes,(uint256,address,address)))":{"params":{"cva":"The Credit Valuation Adjustment value. In the system, either partyA or partyB can get liquidated and CVA is the penalty that the \t\t\tliquidated side should pay to the other one","deadline":"The user should set a deadline for their request. If no PartyB takes action on the quote within this timeframe, the request will expire","lf":"Liquidation Fee. It is the prize that will be paid to the liquidator user","maxFundingRate":"The maximum funding rate allowed from user side.","orderType":"Can be LIMIT or MARKET (0 or 1)","partyAmm":"The partyA Maintenance Margin value. The amount that is actually behind the position and is considered in liquidation status","partyBmm":"The partyB Maintenance Margin value. The amount that is actually behind the position and is considered in liquidation status","partyBsWhiteList":"List of party B addresses allowed to act on this quote.","positionType":"Can be SHORT or LONG (0 or 1)","price":"For limit orders, this is the user-requested price for the position, and for market orders, this acts as the price threshold \t\t\t\tthat the user is willing to open a position. For example, if the market price for an arbitrary symbol is $1000 and the user wants to \t\t\t\to